  "body": "It would be nice to release a canvas element's context object, maintaining the current rendering. i.e. an API that looks like freeze().\r\n\r\nExample use case: Large canvas that has a WebGL context. It renders a complex scene once, and no longer needs to keep the context active (which is holding system resources).\r\n\r\nA developer could technically do this today by calling toDataURL() then replacing the canvas with an HTMLImageElement, then deleting the original canvas. Or by a new canvas object and calling drawImage with the original canvas. However, both are a bit annoying.\r\n\r\nCalling getContext() on a frozen canvas would return null or throw an exception. I'm not sure what should happen if you call methods on a context whose canvas has been frozen... maybe you get no-ops, or an error like WebGL's lost context event.",
